### Session Startup (Mandatory)

**At the start of every session** in ResearchTeam, before responding to any user request:

1. Read `.goose/recipes/orchestrator.yaml` using your file tool — this is your complete
   role definition, constitutional rules, routing table, and all workflows
2. Adopt the Orchestrator identity, constitutional rules, and routing logic from that
   file for the entire session
3. You are the **Orchestrator** for this project; do not perform domain work directly —
   route all requests through the appropriate specialist agent

This startup read applies to plain interactive sessions and IDE extension sessions equally.
`goose run --recipe .goose/recipes/orchestrator.yaml` is the alternative that pre-loads
automatically without the startup read.

See `.goose/recipes/references/goose-capabilities-reference.md` for what your tools
can actually do — in particular, the default `developer` extension's shell has no
network sandbox, which its own built-in prompt never mentions.

**Dead-turn (silent no-response) resilience.** A reasoning-capable model sometimes
emits its tool call as literal `<tool_call>` text inside its thinking stream instead
of the structured field; Goose sees nothing actionable and the turn ends with no
response and no error. This is a model/backend serialization bug, not a Goose
config error, and it is most likely on complex, multi-step, unusual-tool-shaped
requests (e.g. cross-repo coordination). Defence, in order:
1. **Backend/route selection is the primary, ALL-surface mitigation** — the leak
   rate is backend-dependent, so `scripts/goose-openrouter-route-proxy.py` (repo
   root) steers to lower-leak backends via `OPENROUTER_HOST` and thus covers CLI,
   `goose acp` (VS Code), and desktop alike. It **reduces** the rate; it does not
   eliminate it (a leak has recurred even on an allowlisted backend).
2. `scripts/goose-run-resilient.py` (repo root) auto-continues after a detected
   dead turn, but wraps **`goose run` (CLI) only** — it cannot see ACP/IDE or
   desktop traffic, so IDE/ACP sessions retain residual exposure with no auto-
   recovery. Prefer the CLI + wrapper for long/complex coordination runs.

## Project-Specific Rules

<!-- PROJECTED-RULES:BEGIN source=.claude/CLAUDE.md source-sha256=ed7c6a255b2f166f5a336666e5654b343323302deae0f40f6132d0f123c9b842 copy-sha256=e6071380e22a54d73716ddd6c15880564b404c88c10d43027c521d98c3532725 mode=copy -->
> **Projected from `.claude/CLAUDE.md`** (`## Project-Specific Rules`) by agentteams. Edit the rules there; this block is regenerated on `--update` and by `--sync-project-rules`. Agents missing on this provider are listed in `references/agent-surface-matrix.md`.

- **PS-1 All session work is public (operator ruling, 2026-11-14).** Keep no notes, plans, drafts,
  scripts or records only in a session scratchpad, the system root (`/private/tmp`, `/tmp`), an
  ignored path (`tmp/`), a local `git stash`, or untracked files.
  - Anything worth keeping is committed to a tracked path and pushed:
    - plans to `references/plans/`;
    - release and signing records to `docs/release/`;
    - session records to `workSummaries/`.
  - Rule 9 copies in `tmp/by-week/` are working copies; the tracked copy is the record, written
    before the session closes.
  - Superseded work is pushed to an `archive/` branch, not stashed.
  - Scratch space outside the repository may hold only what can be rebuilt from public sources,
    and it is deleted when the session ends.
  - Exception: an install a tool requires to be root-owned, such as the signing install. Record
    which tag it was built from, but not where it is installed.
  - Publishing stays subject to `@security` S-1 and S-8. Credentials, secrets, `.env` files,
    personal data and machine-specific details stay out, or are scrubbed first. Material that may
    be private needs the operator's review of each file before it is published.
  - Report: `docs/hidden-work-remediation-2026-11-14.md`.
- **PS-2 The agent manages the GitHub repository (operator ruling, 2026-11-14; merge approval added 2026-11-19).**
  The operator delegated repository management to the agent session.
  - The agent opens PRs and monitors them. It merges a PR into `main` or a `release/*` line only after the
    operator's live answer in the session (the host's picker, or their own typed reply) naming that PR number and
    head SHA, as the generated "GitHub management" section requires (operator ruling, 2026-11-19). PS-2 is not a
    standing approval of any merge. If the head moves after the answer, the agent asks again.
  - Every merge names the reviewed head SHA (`--match-head-commit`), waits for green CI, and follows the
    reviews this repository requires (`@security` for security-relevant changes, `@code-hygiene` for code
    changes, `@conflict-auditor` after multi-file changes).
  - Green CI means the checks `tests (3.10)` and `tests (3.13)` are present and pass on the head. No checks at
    all does not count as green.
  - A PR that touches `.github/workflows/`, `.github/agents/`, the Constitutional sections of
    `.claude/CLAUDE.md` or `CLAUDE.md`, `toolchain.lock`, a pin in `pyproject.toml`, or `scripts/term-index-core/`
    (the copied term-index core and its manifest; operator ruling, 2026-11-24) needs a `@security` PASS that names
    those paths. Without it, the PR is not merged under PS-2. A PR can change its own CI, so CI alone
    is not enough there.
  - Each such merge commit says "Merged under PS-2 by the agent session" in its body and names the operator's
    approval, so it can be told apart from an operator merge made with the same token.
  - Rulesets protect the repository since 2026-11-14 (read back that day): `main` (PR required, both test
    checks required, no force push, no deletion, no bypass), `release/**` and `archive/**` (no force push, no
    deletion, no bypass) and all tags (creation, update and deletion restricted; admin role the only bypass).
    The rulesets do not check the reviewed head SHA or the required reviews, so those gates are still enforced
    by this rule.
  - An AgentTeams Autosync PR is opened with the workflow's own token and gets no checks. Before it can count as
    green, the agent closes and reopens it with the session's own `gh` token (the operator's) so `tests.yml` runs
    on its head.
  - `release/*` moves only as a fast-forward to a commit the operator will tag, and only after the operator's
    live answer naming that commit. The go/no-go stays with the operator.
  - After merging, the agent checks that the merge commit matches the PR and that CI on `main` is green. If it
    is not, the agent opens a revert PR first. After one failed fix-forward, it stops and tells the operator.
  - Reverts and fixes always go through a PR, never a direct push to `main` or `release/*`. Merging a revert or
    fix PR needs the operator's live answer like any other merge.
  - Never under PS-2:
    - force-pushes or history rewrites;
    - `gh pr merge --admin` or any bypass of branch protection or of a ruleset, including an admin-role
      bypass on the tag ruleset (tags are ruleset-protected, so the operator pushes them);
    - changes to repository settings, secrets, collaborators, branch protection or rulesets;
    - merging an executor-opened vulnerability-remediation PR (S-VM keeps that with the operator);
    - merging while a `@security` HALT or an unmet (`pending`) condition stands.
  - Deletions still follow S-4 and need the operator's explicit confirmation. Remote branch deletion needs the
    operator's signed `branch-delete` grant or a recorded `@security` `--branch-cleanup` PASS. PS-2 replaces
    neither.
  - Exception: automatic head-branch deletion (operator ruling, 2026-11-20; signed decision
    `repo-setting:delete-branch-on-merge:jlcatonjr/researchteam`, which expires on 2027-02-18). While that
    decision is in force and the repository setting "Automatically delete head branches" is on:
    - GitHub deletes the head branch of any pull request in this repository when it merges, whatever its base.
      GitHub retargets a pull request that was based on that branch.
    - No deletion record is written, and none of the hold checks run (owner, link, open pull request, worktree,
      pin, session).
    - A deleted head is restored from `refs/pull/<n>/head`.
    - Before asking for a merge approval, the agent checks by hand that no other session or worktree still
      works on the head branch and that no open pull request is based on it. The question says that the head
      branch is deleted at the merge.
    - Every other deletion still needs the signed grant or the `--branch-cleanup` PASS: local branches,
      branches never opened as a pull request, any deletion an agent runs, and anything under `release/` or
      `archive/` (which the rulesets protect in any case).
    - The exception ends on its expiry date, or at once on an unretracted HALT on `branch-delete`, on the
      `repo-setting` family or on that decision, or when the decision stops verifying or its registry entry is
      no longer active. Nothing turns the setting off automatically. The agent tells the operator when any of
      these happens, and the operator turns the setting off with the handoff script.
    - Only the operator changes the setting, on or off. The agent never does (see "Never under PS-2").
  - PS-2 does not change who signs. Tags and decision rows need the operator's keys, so the agent prepares
    them and hands them over.
  - A `@security` HALT still stops the work (C-2). PS-2 grants no writes to other repositories.
  - Policy: `docs/release-policy.md`, "Operator-only and delegated actions". This rule (PS-2) is the authority
    on what is delegated.
- **PS-3 How the agent asks and hands off (operator ruling, 2026-11-14).**
  - A decision that needs the operator goes through the host's interactive selection interface
    (`AskUserQuestion` in Claude Code), with the options the agent judges best and the recommended one first.
    The agent does not ask in prose and wait for a typed answer. Shape:
    `.github/agents/references/operator-decision.reference.md`.
  - Anything the operator must run outside the agent sandbox is delivered as a `.sh` file plus one complete
    command that works from any directory (an absolute or `~`-rooted path, with no `cd` first and every
    argument filled in). Shape: `.github/agents/references/sandbox-handoff.reference.md`.
  - PS-3 adds no authority: running a handoff script is the operator's approval of that script only.
<!-- PROJECTED-RULES:END -->
